# TEMPLATE — a vLLM variant (DGX Spark, NVIDIA Linux). Copy it, don't start
# from a blank file. Mac and Windows variants serve with llama.cpp:
# TEMPLATE-llamacpp.env.
#
# Every model is different. The QUESTIONS are not. This file is the eight
# questions in the order that matters, because each one can veto the next:
# there is no point choosing a tool-call parser for a model that doesn't fit.
#
#   1 FIT        does it physically fit, and on how many nodes?
#   2 ENGINE     does something exist that runs it on THIS hardware?
#   3 ACCESS     can I actually get the weights?
#   4 SHAPE      the flags that make it fit and run
#   5 DIALECT    the flags that make its output CORRECT
#   6 ENV        what the hardware and fabric demand
#   7 PATCH      what upstream hasn't landed yet
#   8 PROOF      how I know it works, and the number
#
# Answer them in order. Write the answer AND the evidence — a flag without a
# reason is a flag nobody can safely delete later. If you don't know, say
# "unknown" in a comment and leave the flag out. An honest gap beats a
# cargo-culted value; the gap gets closed by a measurement, the cargo cult
# never does.
#
# Rule that keeps this file short: if it is true for EVERY model, it does not
# belong in a recipe. It belongs in launch-solo.sh / launch-cluster.sh / .env.
# A variant of another recipe sources it and states only what differs; it is
# short because everything else was already true somewhere else.
#
# A recipe is a folder, recipes/<name>/: model.env holds what every platform
# shares (MODEL, ROLES, the DIALECT_* a client needs), and one file per
# platform sources it. `rack new <name> <org/model> --dgx` (or --linux) writes
# model.env and copies this file from the "# --- 1 FIT" line down into
# <name>/dgx.env. Everything above that line is guidance for you and never
# reaches a scaffolded recipe — so keep explanation up here and keep the slots
# below fillable. `rack recipes check` says what a recipe may contain.


# --- 1 FIT ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Run this BEFORE writing anything below:
#     rack fit <org/model>
# Record what it said. Bytes on disk vs (usable x nodes - KV reserve).
#   download   ___ GB across ___ shards
#   params     ___ B total / ___ B active
#   verdict    1 node: ___    2 nodes: ___
# If it only fits at a lower precision, that is a SHAPE decision (see 4) and
# the fit above is the wrong number — redo it against the quantized size.
WEIGHTS_GB=                      # the download, in GB: rack recipes --<platform> says what fits

# --- 2 ENGINE ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The question is not "does vLLM support this model", it is "does a build that
# supports this model exist FOR sm_121 / aarch64". Those are different dates.
# Leave IMAGE unset to use the repo default; set it when the model needs a
# different engine entirely (a diffusion model on vllm-omni, say).
#   supported since   vLLM ___ / PR #___
#   image chosen      ___                because ___
ENGINE=vllm
# IMAGE=

# --- 3 ACCESS ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Gated? Which partition? A repo can be 500 GB while the part you need is 144.
# Note the exact download command that got the right subset — future you will
# not re-derive it. MODEL itself is in model.env, shared by every platform.
. "$RECIPE_DIR/model.env"

# --- 4 SHAPE ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The flags that decide whether it fits and starts. These are SIZING knobs,
# not speed knobs — raising them does not make it faster, it makes it OOM.
# The two that trade against each other: --max-model-len and --max-num-seqs.
# Never raise both.
SERVE_ARGS=(
  # rack up sets --host, --port, --served-model-name and the API key.

  # fit
  # --quantization               # if the fit in (1) depended on it, it is MANDATORY here
  # --tensor-parallel-size 2      # only if it does not fit on one node
  --gpu-memory-utilization 0.8   # 0.8 is near the edge on 128 GB unified
  --max-model-len FILL_ME        # these two share one KV pool. Decide which the
  --max-num-seqs FILL_ME         # workload needs and starve the other.
  # --kv-cache-dtype fp8         # some MLA layouts REJECT 'auto' — check the error text

  # throughput, safe defaults
  --enable-prefix-caching
  --enable-chunked-prefill
  --async-scheduling

  # --- 5 DIALECT ------------------------------------------------------------
  # Chat models only — a diffusion/omni engine has no dialect layer and drops
  # this whole section. If that is you, delete it rather than
  # leaving commented flags nobody can explain later.
  # The flags that make the output CORRECT rather than merely present. Skipping
  # these does not crash — it silently returns wrong-shaped answers, which is
  # worse. Check the model's tokenizer file in vLLM, not just its model card:
  # defaults there have disagreed with the vendor's hosted API (see dsv4.env,
  # where thinking is on by default upstream and OFF in vLLM).
  # --trust-remote-code          # if it ships custom tokenizer/config code
  # --tokenizer-mode
  # --enable-auto-tool-choice
  # --tool-call-parser
  # --reasoning-parser
  # Per-REQUEST settings are not flags: they are DIALECT_* in model.env, where
  # the app reads them (thinking switch, effort, what to echo back).

  # --- 6 ENV, part one: compile ---------------------------------------------
  # PIECEWISE is the default answer on cross-node GB10. FULL_AND_PIECEWISE
  # compiled 4.75 h without converging (measured 2026-07-31).
  # Diagnostic-only next rung down: --enforce-eager
  --compilation-config '{"cudagraph_mode":"PIECEWISE","custom_ops":["all"]}'
  # --init-timeout / --stage-init-timeout — raise if load is slow; H3 lost to
  # the 600 s default by twelve seconds and died READY.
)

# --- 6 ENV, part two --------------------------------------------------------
# What the hardware and fabric demand, not what the model wants. NOTE: both
# halves of question 6 are SITE answers, not model answers — they are the
# lines you re-derive when this recipe moves to different hardware (a rented
# GB300/RTX Pro box: PIECEWISE and LAMPORT_RS_SCONV are Spark/RoCE scars,
# not portable truths). See docs/07 Part 11.
ENV_EXTRA=(
  # LAMPORT_RS_SCONV=0           # MANDATORY on RoCE for NVLink-assuming collectives
  # locality defaults (HF_HUB_OFFLINE etc.) are already baked into the launchers
)

# --- 7 PATCH ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Only when upstream hasn't landed it. Every entry needs three things:
# what it fixes, the upstream ref, and the CONDITION UNDER WHICH IT IS DELETED.
# A mod with no deletion condition becomes permanent by accident.
MODS=()        # e.g. mods/inkling-sm12-paged-kv — delete when flash-attention#2348 lands

# --- 8 PROOF ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Not "it started". A number, and how you got it.
#     rack up <name> && rack bench <label>
#   decode      ___ tok/s median
#   TTFT        ___ ms
#   KV pool     ___ tokens
#   peak mem    ___ GB          (free -h — nvidia-smi is blind on unified memory)
# If no published number exists for this model on this hardware, say so. That
# makes yours the first one.
